Analyze the reference swaps in the code below. What is the console output?

```java
class NodePair {
    int val;
    NodePair(int v) { this.val = v; }
}
public class Main {
    static void swap(NodePair a, NodePair b) {
        NodePair temp = a;
        a = b;
        b = temp;
        a.val = 500;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        NodePair p1 = new NodePair(10);
        NodePair p2 = new NodePair(20);
        swap(p1, p2);
        System.out.println(p1.val + " " + p2.val);
    }
}
```

Trace the shallow copy behavior in the following program. What output is produced?

```java
class Payload {
    int amount = 5;
}
class Packet implements Cloneable {
    Payload payload = new Payload();
    public Packet shallowCopy() {
        try {
            return (Packet) super.clone();
        } catch (CloneNotSupportedException e) {
            return null;
        }
    }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Packet original = new Packet();
        Packet duplicate = original.shallowCopy();
        duplicate.payload.amount = 99;
        System.out.println(original.payload.amount + " " + (original.payload == duplicate.payload));
    }
}
```

Examine how string pooling and intern() interact in this snippet. What is printed?

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String lit = "Echo";
        String constructed = new String("Echo");
        String interned = constructed.intern();
        System.out.print((lit == constructed) + " ");
        System.out.print((lit == interned) + " ");
        System.out.println(constructed == interned);
    }
}
```

